.TH SYSLOGAPP 1 "October 2026" "SysLogApp Preview 41" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
syslogapp \- terminal-style journal and network viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B syslogapp
.SH DESCRIPTION
SysLogApp is a compact GTK3 journal viewer with current-boot/history, severity,
service and local text filters. Select a record to inspect the complete message.
The viewer runs as the current user; journal access depends on user permissions.
.PP
The initial view is a snapshot. Enable Live to follow new events. Hold scroll
suspends automatic scrolling while collection continues. Retained history and
pending updates are bounded. During overload, skipped pending records are counted
in the status. Log viewing never modifies the on-disk journal.
.SH HOSTS
The collapsible Hosts sidebar selects Local or saved SSH profiles. Password and
SSH key/agent login are supported; passwords/passphrases and key contents are
never saved. Unknown host keys require confirmation; changed keys are rejected.
Reconnect retries login; Disconnect returns to Local. A single active OpenSSH
multiplexed session supplies all four tabs. Switching closes old readers/session,
frees old pages and ignores late callbacks. No profiles auto-connect at startup.
.PP
All commands use the selected host. Remote System logs requires journalctl and
journal access; Network needs ip/ss; Overview and rotation helpers need Python 3.8+.
Missing tools disable features or report Unavailable without using local data.
Remote administrative actions use sudo/root and target-specific confirmation.
Host changes and closing are blocked during maintenance. A failed SSH transport
can leave a remote administrative operation running; inspect before retrying.
Remote journal snapshots have a 16 MiB output limit. Journal timestamps are shown
in the client's local timezone. Host profiles are stored with normal app settings.
.SH NETWORK
The Network tab shows local interfaces, addresses, default routes and TCP/UDP
sockets. It collects once when first opened. Refresh collects on demand; Live enables
automatic updates approximately every two seconds while visible. Pause holds
the snapshot, Resume restarts updates, and hiding the tab suspends polling.
Reads never overlap; collection errors cause backoff up to 30 seconds. Search and
filters operate on loaded data. Hide TIME-WAIT optionally hides closed sockets
awaiting expiry, without another scan. It combines with search/type filters and
stays applied across refresh/live updates while the app is open. Processes enables additional process lookup on
the next refresh. Process visibility depends on user permissions.
.PP
Network snapshots are bounded to 1000 sockets and 128 interfaces. Each collection
command has a 3-second timeout and a 512 KiB output budget. Numeric addresses avoid
DNS lookups. No external reachability probes run. No network timer runs when Live is off,
paused or the Network tab is hidden.
Columns are grouped as Proto/State, Local address/port, Remote address/port
and optional Process/PID at the end. Flat headers identify Local address, Local
port, Remote address and Remote port. Columns remain separately sortable and resizable. Both address columns fit their loaded data with trailing
spare space, keeping each port nearby. Manual widths take precedence. Copy socket
follows the visual column order. All Network headers and values align left,
including wrapped text. Column widths
fit the window and text wraps as needed. Active connections are blue, listeners
purple, idle/closing sockets gray and CLOSE-WAIT amber. Ordinary endpoint text
uses quiet colors. Ports are amber, PIDs are blue and process names are purple.
PID and Process are separate columns. Extended processes optionally shows full
loaded socket metadata including descriptors; it performs no additional scan.
Tooltips and Copy socket retain complete process metadata.
.SH OVERVIEW AND MAINTENANCE
Overview loads a local system snapshot on first opening and manual Refresh only.
It shows system identity, uptime, CPU/load, memory/swap and root filesystem usage.
It does not sample CPU percentages or run background checks.
.PP
Maintenance actions run only when selected. Changes require confirmation and
pkexec administrator authentication. Missing optional tools disable their actions.
Administrative operations use a separate worker without a fixed timeout. Keep the
app open until completion; window closing is blocked while an action runs.
Command output is temporarily stored on disk; only the last 128 KiB is displayed.
Read-only journal usage has a 5-second timeout and 64 KiB output limit.
Ctrl+R on Maintenance rechecks executable availability on the selected host.
.SH TEXT LOG ROTATION
Maintenance includes a logrotate editor for one regular file under /var/log.
Choose schedule, retained archives, compression, size threshold and rename or
copytruncate. Rename requires the writer to reopen; no reopen hook is added.
Copytruncate can lose concurrent writes. The system timer/cron job controls when
rotation settings are evaluated.
.PP
Saving requires review and pkexec authentication. The helper validates a staged
full configuration with logrotate --debug, backs up previous app-managed settings
under /var/backups/syslogapp and atomically saves /etc/logrotate.d/syslogapp.
Validation failures and duplicate rules leave configuration unchanged. Saving does
not rotate logs. Distro-owned rules are not edited. Logrotate is optional.
.SH RESIZING
Drag header dividers in Logs, Network and Overview to resize columns. Fit columns
restores automatic window fitting. Text rewraps after dragging; manual widths
survive refresh, window resizing and app restarts and may require horizontal scrolling.
Window size, maximized state and per-tab manual widths are saved atomically on
normal close. Unopened tabs retain their saved widths. Fit columns clears saved
overrides for that tab. No settings are written continuously while resizing.
Overview displays a built-in ASCII logo without running neofetch.
.SH SORTING
Click a table header to sort; click again to reverse. Logs open oldest first, with newest entries last.
Time sorting uses full journal timestamps, and severity sorting uses journal
priority. Network addresses and ports sort numerically. State sorting groups
established connections, listeners, handshakes, closing and unconnected sockets.
Selected sorts survive refresh and live updates. Enabling Live restores ascending
Time order and jumps to the newest visible entry at the bottom. Interfaces and routes can be
expanded above the socket table. Full timestamps remain in tooltips and details.
.SH RESOURCE USE
Network refreshes retain unchanged table rows instead of rebuilding the table.
Sorting computes tie-break fields only when needed. Long strings bypass global
sort caches; retained messages are not truncated. Date parsing caches are bounded.
No new background sampling or refresh timers are introduced.
.SH KEYBOARD
Ctrl+K or Ctrl+F focuses search. Ctrl+R refreshes the selected tab. Escape closes event details.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/syslogapp/config.json
The log_lines preference defaults to 400 and is clamped to 100 through 2000.
